      "question": {
        ":question/id": "34258f0c-d3eb-58e8-aa92-01657f0a3609",
        ":question/problem": "Find $k$, if $4{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2}=0$ and\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $k$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 4\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+k\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ k\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ k\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -16\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the first components, we get\n\n$$\n4k=-8\\,\u27f9\\,k=-2.\n$$\n\nBut then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\ne \\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -16\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there is no $k$ such that $4{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2}=0.$",
